<Page
    x:Class="Zink.Pages.PrivacyPolicyPage"
    xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml/presentation"
    xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml"
    Background="{ThemeResource ApplicationPageBackgroundThemeBrush}">

    <ScrollViewer Padding="20">
        <StackPanel>
            <TextBlock Text="Privacy Policy" FontSize="24" FontWeight="Bold" Margin="0,0,0,20"/>

            <TextBlock TextWrapping="Wrap">
Privacy Policy for Zink

Zink ("we", "our", or "us") is a media, browsing, calling, recording, and streaming hub for Windows. This policy explains what data Zink uses, why it is used, and when data may leave your device.

1. Information Zink Uses

- Account and authentication data:
  Some features use third-party sign-in, such as Spotify or Zink social/calling services. Zink stores access tokens locally so you do not need to sign in every time. Zink does not store your Spotify password.

- Spotify integration:
  Spotify features use Spotify's OAuth and Web API services for playback, library, device, and saved-track features. Spotify account data is handled according to Spotify's privacy policy at https://www.spotify.com/privacy/.

- Web browsing and third-party web services:
  Zink Connect and the app's web pages can open third-party services such as YouTube, Spotify, Discord, Netflix, TikTok, X, Twitch, and other websites. Those services may collect data according to their own privacy policies and terms. Zink does not claim ownership of those third-party services.

- Browser history and browsing data:
  Zink Connect can store local browsing history and WebView data on your device for convenience. You can turn browser history off and clear browsing data from the app settings.

- Ad blocking and content filtering:
  Zink Connect includes ad-blocking and filtering functionality to improve the browsing experience. Filtering is applied locally inside the Zink browser experience where supported.

- Microphone and audio data:
  Microphone access is used only for user-started features such as voice calls, screen recording with microphone audio, and live streaming. Zink does not record microphone audio unless you start a feature that uses it.

- Screen capture and recording data:
  Screen capture is used only when you choose to start screen sharing, screen recording, FPS recording, or streaming. Captured video is saved locally or sent to the service you choose, such as a call or stream destination.

- Local media files and folders:
  Zink accesses music, video, or folder content only after you choose files or folders through Windows picker dialogs or file associations. Zink does not automatically scan your entire device.

- Diagnostics and support reports:
  Zink can create diagnostic logs and support bundles to help troubleshoot calls, screen sharing, streaming, and playback. Uploads happen only when you choose an upload action. If an upload fails, the report may be saved locally on your device.

2. How Zink Uses Data

- To play local and online media.
- To connect to third-party services that you choose to use.
- To enable calls, screen sharing, recording, streaming, and diagnostics.
- To remember local preferences such as theme, history settings, media libraries, and playback state.

3. Permissions

- Internet access:
  Required for streaming media, web browsing, radio, account sign-in, third-party services, diagnostics upload, and real-time calling.

- Microphone:
  Required for voice calls, microphone recording, and live streaming when you enable microphone audio.

- Graphics capture:
  Required for screen sharing, screen recording, FPS recording, and streaming. Capture begins only after you choose or start a capture feature.

- Global media control:
  Used to detect and display currently playing media from compatible apps, such as Spotify, so Zink can show playback status and controls.

- File access:
  Used only for files and folders you select, files you open with Zink, and media output locations you choose.

4. Third-Party Services

Zink may connect to third-party services including Spotify, Discord, YouTube, Twitch, X, TikTok, Netflix, Amazon, Microsoft/Xbox, and other websites displayed inside the app. Each third-party service remains responsible for its own content, accounts, subscriptions, terms, and privacy practices.

5. User Control

- You can stop calls, recording, streaming, and screen sharing at any time.
- You can clear local browsing data and disable browser history.
- You can revoke app permissions in Windows Settings.
- You can sign out of third-party services through those services or by clearing local WebView data.
- Zink does not sell your personal data.

6. Contact

If you have questions or concerns about privacy, contact:
